Well, first of all, you'd have to be a giant humanoid in order to pick
up any boulders (correct me if I'm wrong, but I don't think any other
creatures can pick up boulders); and you'd have to remain a giant for
a long time while going back and visiting old levels.  That's a pretty
limiting factor.

But more importantly, if I understand you right, you're not proposing
a change in how rocks can be picked up and turned to gold; just how
golden boulders/statues can be broken up into gold.  So I could still
just break up the boulders and statues with my pick-axe, and THEN pick
up the resulting rocks and turn them into gold.  So there's still no
magic required.  Magic would only be needed to break up aurified
monsters.  That's fine with me...

But just to limit things a little more, how about this: anything the
player *wields* is turned to gold (in addition to anything the player
picks up).  That would eliminate the use of pick-axes to break up
boulders; the pick-axe would turn to gold.

I think that as above, the Midas Touch is a bad enough curse,
and is difficult to turn into a net advantage, as long as it
cannot be turned on and off at will.  So something like a
potion of hallucination would be a good model.  (But could a unicorn
horn cure the Midas Touch?  Certainly praying could...)

One thing to think about is whether it is feasible to simply
turn existing objects into gold versions of those objects.
Note that the "material" field already has a value of "GOLD"
for some objects.  Thus picking up a gold ring, for example,
should not affect it.  (Unless we define the "Midas Touch" in
Nethack to mean "everything you touch turns to zorkmids", which
is an alternative.)
What should happen if you pick up an iron sword?
I think that currently, the "material" field is stored on a
per-object-type basis; i.e. you couldn't set a particular
long sword's "material" field to "GOLD" without making all
long swords gold (if you could do that at all).  So I see
three options:
1) add a one-bit composed_of_gold field to all objects;
2) add a "hunk of gold" object;
3) just turn any object picked up into gold pieces (zorkmids).

1 is undesirable because of the increase in the size of objects; just
think if every new feature added a bit to the object size.  Another
problem with this is that you then have to modify many, many routines
that manipulate objects, to check whether the object is gold, and
if so, possibly change the behavior of the object.  For example,
golden food could not be eaten; a golden pick-axe would, I guess,
be unusable; a golden scroll could probably be read, and would
not be burned by fire (but might melt into a blank scroll); etc.

2 seems pretty good; maybe the "hunk of gold" object would contain
a pointer to the object type of what the thing used to be.  That
way we could have the name of the object be printed as
"a +1 golden pick-axe", "a golden tin of spinach" etc.; but
for all purposes we could treat it as a useless lump.
Then there would need to be routines for doing something with
the lump such as breaking it up by magical means into zorkmids
-- that's where the magical limitation comes in!  Also, gold
pieces exposed to fire could be fused into a "lump of gold" (with
no object type pointer) which has to be broken apart magically
to get the gold back.

3 would be the easiest to program but would be kind of cheesy.

